The '''2010 Football League Cup final''' was the final match of the [[2009–10 Football League Cup]], the 50th season of the [[Football League Cup]], a [[association football|football]] competition for the 92 teams in the [[Premier League]] and [[The Football League]]. The match, played at [[Wembley Stadium]] on 28 February 2010,<ref>{{cite news|title=Key Dates Revealed |url=http://www.football-league.co.uk/page/News/CarlingCupNews/0,,10794~1597362,00.html |work=football-league.co.uk |publisher=The Football League |date=20 March 2009 |access-date=10 August 2009 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090814174109/http://www.football-league.co.uk/page/News/CarlingCupNews/0%2C%2C10794~1597362%2C00.html |archive-date=14 August 2009 }}</ref> was won by [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]], who beat [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]] 2–1. Aston Villa took the lead in the fifth minute of the game, via a [[James Milner]] [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty kick]], but [[Michael Owen]] equalised for Manchester United seven minutes later. [[Wayne Rooney]], who replaced the injured Owen shortly before half time, scored the winning goal with 16 minutes left to play.<ref>{{cite news |first=Henry |last=Winter |title=Aston Villa 1 Manchester United 2: Carling Cup final match report |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/manchester-united/7338947/Aston-Villa-1-Manchester-United-2-Carling-Cup-final-match-report.html |work=Telegraph.co.uk |publisher=Telegraph Media Group |date=28 February 2010 |access-date=1 March 2010 }}</ref>

Manchester United went into the match as defending champions, having beaten [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] on penalties in the [[2009 Football League Cup final|2009 final]]. The win gave them their fourth Football League Cup title, their third in five years and their second in succession, becoming the first team to retain the trophy since [[Nottingham Forest F.C.|Nottingham Forest]] in [[1990 Football League Cup final|1990]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Kevin |last=McCarra |title=Wayne Rooney seals Manchester United's Carling Cup comeback |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/feb/28/aston-villa-manchester-united-carling-cup-final |work=guardian.co.uk |publisher=Guardian News and Media |date=28 February 2010 |access-date=1 March 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100330060019/http://www.guardian.co.uk/football/2010/feb/28/aston-villa-manchester-united-carling-cup-final |archive-date=30 March 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref> It was the first time Manchester United successfully defended a major cup, having been losing finalists as title holders in the [[1995 FA Cup final|1995]] and [[2005 FA Cup final|2005]] FA Cup, and the [[2009 UEFA Champions League Final|2009]] Champions League finals.<ref>{{cite news|title=Rooney the hero as United overcome Villa |url=http://espnfc.com/en/report/287675/report.html |publisher=ESPN FC |date=28 February 2010 |access-date=4 January 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140118091449/http://espnfc.com/en/report/287675/report.html |archive-date=18 January 2014 }}</ref>  Since Manchester United qualified for the [[2010–11 UEFA Champions League]] via their league position, the place in the [[2010–11 UEFA Europa League|following season]]'s [[UEFA Europa League|Europa League]] which would have been given to the winners of the League Cup was instead given to Villa, since they finished in sixth place in the [[2009–10 Premier League]].

==Background==
Out of the 173 previous meetings between the two sides, Manchester United had won 87 and Aston Villa had won 49, with the remaining 37 games finishing as draws; however, Villa's record in the League Cup against United was markedly better, with four wins in six matches between them.<ref name="meetings">{{cite web |title=United versus Aston Villa |url=http://www.stretfordend.co.uk/opponents/astonvilla.html |publisher=StretfordEnd.co.uk |access-date=5 February 2010 }}</ref> United's only League Cup win over Aston Villa came in October 1975, when they won 2–1 at [[Villa Park]] in the third round.<ref name="meetings"/> The only previous meeting between the two teams in the final of the competition came in [[1994 Football League Cup final|1994]], when Villa won 3–1 and United winger [[Andrei Kanchelskis]] became the first player to be sent off in a League Cup final.

Aston Villa hold the advantage over Manchester United in the league matches between the two sides in 2009–10, having beaten the Premier League champions 1–0 at [[Old Trafford]] in December and holding them to a 1–1 draw at [[Villa Park]] three weeks before the League Cup final.<ref>{{cite news |first=Ian |last=Hughes |title=Man Utd 0–1 Aston Villa |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/8402301.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=12 December 2009 |access-date=28 February 2010 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Aston Villa 1–1 Man Utd |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/8501875.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=10 February 2010 |access-date=28 February 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160112215850/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/8501875.stm |archive-date=12 January 2016 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Both Manchester United and Aston Villa had played in seven Football League Cup finals, but Manchester United had only won three compared to Villa's five. United's most recent victory, however, came in [[2009 Football League Cup final|2009]] – when they beat Tottenham Hotspur on [[penalty shootout (association football)|penalties]] – whereas Villa's last League Cup title came in [[1996 Football League Cup final|1996]], when they beat [[Leeds United F.C.|Leeds United]] 3–0. Manchester United went into the match looking to become the first team to retain the League Cup since Nottingham Forest in [[1990 Football League Cup final|1990]].

